    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephones in the owner-create/update flow.\n\n**Change:** `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS` table mapping country calling code \u2192 required national-digit count (`\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`).\n- After `normalizeTelephone` builds the E.164 string and passes the generic 8\u201315 digit check, it now calls a new `validateNationalNumberLength(...)` helper.\n- The helper strips the leading `+`, matches the **longest** known country code prefix (so `+61` beats `+6`/`+1`), and rejects when the remaining national digits don't match the required length. Country codes not in the table fall through to the existing generic E.164 rule, preserving prior behavior.\n- Rejection reuses `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, which `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already maps to **HTTP 400**.\n\n**Verification:** The new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posts `+61 123`, expecting 400) passes, and the full suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`). The valid path is unaffected: `uniqueTelephone()` yields `+61` + 9 national digits and `+1` NANP numbers carry 10, both satisfying the new rule.",
